 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# WebApplicationClient.prepare_request_body doesn't include client_secret in request body\n\n## Description\n\nWhen using the WebApplicationClient class to prepare a request body for token requests, the client_secret parameter is not being included in the request body when it should be.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.oauth2 import WebApplicationClient\n\nclient = WebApplicationClient('my_client_id')\n\n# This should include client_secret in the body\nbody = client.prepare_request_body(client_secret='my_client_secret')\nprint(body)  # Expected to see client_secret in the output, but it's missing\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nWhen passing client_secret to prepare_request_body, it should be included in the request body parameters.\n\nAccording to OAuth 2.0 spec, we should be able to:\n1. Include client_id alone in the body (default)\n2. Include client_id and client_secret in auth and not include them in the body (RFC preferred solution)\n3. Include client_id and client_secret in the body (RFC alternative solution)\n4. Include client_id in the body and an empty string for client_secret\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nThe client_secret parameter is being ignored and not included in the request body. This breaks compatibility with OAuth servers that expect the client_secret to be included in the request body.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Python 3.10\n- oauthlib latest version\n\n## Additional context\n\nThis is important for OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow where the client needs to exchange the authorization code for an access token, and the client_secret is required as part of this exchange.\n</issue>\n",
